chore(website): SSR-safety prep — defer browser APIs out of module scope (#107)

Two small refactors that make the existing SPA SSR-prerender-safe.
Independent of which SSG framework we ultimately pick (vite-react-ssg,
vike, react-snap, etc.) — these unblock all of them.

1. ThemeProvider initial state no longer touches localStorage /
   window.matchMedia from useState's initializer (which would
   run on the server during prerender and throw). Extracted to
   initialTheme() with a `typeof window === 'undefined'` guard
   that returns 'dark' on the server. The existing useEffect
   reconciles from real localStorage on hydration, so the
   server default is short-lived. Matches the inline
   `<script>` in index.html that sets data-theme=dark before
   first paint, so no FOUC.

2. Mermaid no longer top-level imports the `mermaid` package.
   The module touches `document` / `window` at module
   evaluation time, which throws under SSG. Converted to a
   dynamic `import('mermaid')` inside an effect; the `mermaid`
   handle gets stored in component state and renderChart()
   short-circuits when it's not ready yet. Type-only import
   from 'mermaid' is kept so the API surface stays typed.

Validation:
  - npx tsc --noEmit clean
  - vitest src/lib src/components/ui → 10/10 pass (existing
    Mermaid + theme tests cover both changes)

Not in this PR (follow-up):
  - The actual SSG framework + prerender pipeline.
    vite-react-ssg 0.9.0 was the first choice, but its peer
    deps still require react-router-dom ^6 and we're on v7.
    Re-evaluating between (a) wait for upstream, (b) Puppeteer-
    based post-build crawler (react-snap shape), (c) custom
    Vite SSR with react 19 renderToPipeableStream, (d) Vike
    migration. Will surface that decision in a separate PR.

